2025-05-01 03:32:58 - Thread-1513 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant tables / columns and why they matter

monthly_sales  
• product_id – lets us isolate the two requested items (4160 and 7790)  
• mth – monthly time‑stamp; needed to (a) choose “first 36 months starting 2016‑01‑01”, (b) map months to time‑step numbers, and (c) split out “seasonality windows” (time‑steps 7 → 30)  
• qty – the actual historical sales volume that the regression / forecast will be built on  

products  
• id – joins to monthly_sales.product_id for readability  
• name – human‑readable product name; optional for result presentation  

Typical filter conditions that will appear  
WHERE product_id IN (4160, 7790)  
AND mth BETWEEN '2016-01-01' AND '2018-12-31'    -- first 36 months  
(and later) AND step_no BETWEEN 7 AND 30          -- seasonality window  

Below are ten exploratory SQL snippets (all with LIMIT 20) that one could run—starting from simple inspection, moving toward the pieces needed for a weighted‑regression forecast.  They purposely do not yet perform the regression itself (that would be done in analytic software once the data have been queried).

```sql
-- 1. Peek at the raw monthly sales rows for product 4160
SELECT DISTINCT *
FROM monthly_sales
WHERE product_id = 4160
ORDER BY mth
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Peek at the raw monthly sales rows for product 7790
SELECT DISTINCT *
FROM monthly_sales
WHERE product_id = 7790
ORDER BY mth
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. List all distinct months we have in the series (useful for step‑number mapping)
SELECT DISTINCT mth
FROM monthly_sales
ORDER BY mth
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Verify we truly have the first 36 months for each product
SELECT product_id,
       COUNT(*)              AS month_cnt,
       MIN(mth)              AS first_mth,
       MAX(mth)              AS last_mth
FROM monthly_sales
WHERE product_id IN (4160, 7790)
  AND mth BETWEEN '2016-01-01' AND '2018-12-31'
GROUP BY product_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Attach readable product names to the sales figures
SELECT ms.product_id,
       p.name               AS product_name,
       ms.mth,
       ms.qty
FROM   monthly_sales AS ms
JOIN   products      AS p  ON p.id = ms.product_id
WHERE  ms.product_id IN (4160, 7790)
  AND  ms.mth BETWEEN '2016-01-01' AND '2018-12-31'
ORDER BY ms.product_id, ms.mth
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Generate a “step number” (1‑based) inside SQLite for the first 36 months
--    (ROW_NUMBER window is available from SQLite 3.25+)
WITH base AS (
  SELECT product_id, mth, qty,
         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
           PARTITION BY product_id
           ORDER BY mth
         ) AS step_no
  FROM monthly_sales
  WHERE product_id IN (4160, 7790)
    AND mth BETWEEN '2016-01-01' AND '2018-12-31'
)
SELECT DISTINCT *
FROM base
ORDER BY product_id, step_no
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Extract the “seasonality window” (steps 7 → 30) for later adjustment‑factor work
WITH base AS (
  SELECT product_id, mth, qty,
         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY product_id ORDER BY mth) AS step_no
  FROM monthly_sales
  WHERE product_id IN (4160, 7790)
    AND mth BETWEEN '2016-01-01' AND '2018-12-31'
)
SELECT DISTINCT product_id, mth, qty, step_no
FROM base
WHERE step_no BETWEEN 7 AND 30
ORDER BY product_id, step_no
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Compute average qty within that seasonality window (quick look)
WITH base AS (
  SELECT product_id, qty,
         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY product_id ORDER BY mth) AS step_no
  FROM monthly_sales
  WHERE product_id IN (4160, 7790)
    AND mth BETWEEN '2016-01-01' AND '2018-12-31'
)
SELECT product_id,
       AVG(qty) AS avg_seasonal_qty
FROM base
WHERE step_no BETWEEN 7 AND 30
GROUP BY product_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Pull the entire 36‑month panel with step numbers for exporting
WITH panel AS (
  SELECT product_id,
         mth,
         qty,
         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY product_id ORDER BY mth) AS step_no
  FROM monthly_sales
  WHERE product_id IN (4160, 7790)
    AND mth BETWEEN '2016-01-01' AND '2018-12-31'
)
SELECT DISTINCT *
FROM panel
ORDER BY product_id, step_no
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Quick annualised total (historical, not forecast) for validation
SELECT product_id,
       SUBSTR(mth, 1, 4) AS yr,
       SUM(qty)          AS qty_year
FROM monthly_sales
WHERE product_id IN (4160, 7790)
  AND mth BETWEEN '2016-01-01' AND '2018-12-31'
GROUP BY product_id, yr
ORDER BY product_id, yr
LIMIT 20;
```

The outputs of these queries give you the 36‑month time series, identify the seasonality slice (steps 7–30), and provide the data needed to run a weighted regression in your preferred analytics tool to forecast 2018 and derive its average annual sales for products 4160 and 7790.
